An antivirus is important because it:
• detects, blocks and removes viruses, worms, Trojans and other malware;
• prevents data loss and damage to the system;
• scans files, emails and downloads before threats enter the computer;
• protects personal and financial information from theft; and
• keeps the computer running safely and efficiently.

It must be updated regularly so that it can fight the latest threats.

Demonstration: restart the computer and press the setup key (usually Del, F2 or F10) during start-up to enter the BIOS/UEFI screen.
In BIOS you can:
• view system information (processor, memory);
• set the date and time;
• change the boot order (which device to start from);
• enable/disable hardware devices and set passwords.
Save the changes with F10 and exit.

A Table of Contents (ToC) lists the headings/chapters of a document together with their page numbers. It helps the reader to:
• quickly locate and jump to a particular section or chapter;
• see the overall structure and organisation of the document at a glance;
• navigate directly to the required page (when the ToC entries are hyperlinks) without scrolling through the whole document.

A watermark is a faint, semi-transparent text or image that appears behind the main text of a document. It is used to:
• identify or authenticate a document;
• show its status or purpose;
• display a company logo or name; and
• discourage unauthorised copying.

Examples: "CONFIDENTIAL", "DRAFT", "SAMPLE", "COPY", "BEST COPY", or a company logo.

A DBMS is preferred over a flat file system because it:
1. reduces data redundancy (duplication) by storing data in related tables;
2. avoids inconsistency, so the same data is not stored in many places;
3. allows data sharing among many users;
4. enforces data integrity, accuracy and security;
5. supports queries, reports and different views of the data;
6. makes updating and retrieving large data easy and reliable.

The data type of a field decides what kind of values can be stored and how much space they take. Choosing the correct data type:
• ensures data is stored correctly (numbers as numbers so they can be calculated, dates as dates);
• prevents wrong entries;
• saves storage space; and
• improves the accuracy and speed of queries and calculations.

An Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) is a graphical plan of a database. In business it is important because it:
• shows the entities, their attributes and the relationships before the database is built;
• helps designers and managers understand the data structure;
• avoids mistakes and redundancy;
• communicates the design clearly to the team; and
• ensures the database meets the business requirements.

